   124	## 3. Phase 1(c) — the effective bound, honestly instantiated
   125	
   126	### 3.1 What the certification criterion actually is
   127	
   128	Contrapositive of effective spreading-out: if V(f) is NONEMPTY over Qbar, then
   129	every "bad" prime p (EMPTY mod p) divides one nonzero integer A(f) with
   130	log A ≤ H1(n, d, h, s). Hence
   131	- **single-prime form**: EMPTY at one p > e^{H1} proves char-0 EMPTY;
   132	- **product form (sharper)**: EMPTY at distinct primes with Σ log p_i > H1
   133	  proves char-0 EMPTY (if all were bad, their product would divide A).
   134	
   135	H1 is governed by worst-case **heights of char-0 witness points** (arithmetic
   136	Bezout/elimination), NOT by any observed cofactor height: our tiny log-2
   137	certificate lives on the empty side and says nothing about the hypothetical
   138	nonempty side that the bound must exclude. Reference shapes:
   139	
   140	- H1_KPS = 4n(n+1)·d^n·(h + log s + (n+7)·log((n+1)d)) — the Krick–Pardo–Sombra
   141	  (Duke 109, 2001, Thm 1) height constant; nonempty-side elimination constants
   142	  (d'Andrea–Krick–Sombra, Ann. Sci. ENS 2013) have the same d^n·(h+…) shape.
   143	- H1_dream = d^n·max(h,1) — an unrealistically generous floor (all
   144	  combinatorial factors deleted). The d^Θ(n)·h shape is REAL, not proof slack:
   145	  iterated-power families (x1 = c, x2 = x1^d, …, xn = x_{n-1}^d) have all
   146	  char-0 points at height ~ d^{n-1}·h, so bad primes of that exponential size
   147	  genuinely occur. No theorem of this type can undercut the shape.
