+
+## 4. Why the Euler terms have zero quadratic loss
+
+### Lemma 4.1 (Euler operators preserve order)
+
+For every field \(k\) and every \(n\ge0\),
+
+\[
+ \theta(t^nR)\subseteq t^nR.                             \tag{4.1}
+\]
+
+Indeed, if \(f\in R\),
+
+\[
+ \theta(t^nf)=t^n(nf+\theta f).                          \tag{4.2}
+\]
+
+After the residue split,
+
+\[
+ \theta\bigl(t^rZ(u)\bigr)=t^r(r+6\Theta)Z(u).           \tag{4.3}
+\]
+
+These formulas remain true in characteristic \(p\). A scalar such as
+\(n\), \(r+6j\), or \(j\) may vanish modulo \(p\), but that can only raise
+order; it cannot lower it.
+
+### Lemma 4.2 (Euler-differential polynomials have \(\sigma=0\))
+
+Let \(\mathcal P\) be a finite polynomial expression, with coefficients of
+nonnegative \(t\)-order, in finitely many series \(x_i\) and their Euler
+derivatives \(\theta^jx_i\). Localizations at fixed chart units are allowed.
+Then the remainder of \(\mathcal P\) about any fixed point satisfies
+(3.2)--(3.3) with \(\sigma=0\).
+
+#### Proof
+
+Expand each monomial after \(x\mapsto x+h\). Subtracting its constant and
+linear parts leaves only terms with at least two factors among
+\(\theta^jh_i\). If \(h\in F^aX\), Lemma 4.1 puts every such factor in
+\(F^a\), while multiplication adds orders. This proves (3.2).
+
+For (3.3), subtract the corresponding remainder at \(h'\) and telescope
+each product. Every resulting term has one factor
+\(\theta^j(h_i-h_i')\in F^m\) and, because the original variation degree
+was at least two, at least one further factor coming from \(h\) or \(h'\),
+of order at least \(a\). All background factors and coefficients have
+nonnegative order. Hence every term has order at least \(a+m\). No
+Taylor factorial or division by the characteristic was used. \(\square\)
+
+The same proof applies to restricted differential power series on an
+\(F^1\)-ball and to inverses of source-dependent units after their convergent
+geometric-series expansion. What is not allowed without accounting for it
+is an unrecorded negative power of \(t\); a normalization losing
+\(\sigma\) orders must use Theorem 3.1 with that \(\sigma\).
+
+The full expression (1.6) is built from products, \(\eta\)-differentiation,
+and \(\theta\). Therefore, once SOURCE-30 exhibits the actual all-depth
+normalized products as integral filtered expressions, Lemma 4.2 proves the
+required quadratic estimate with \(\sigma=0\). The Euler terms cause no
+additional Hensel loss.
+
+Characteristic-\(p\) resonance is instead a **linear inverse** issue. For
+example, on \(k[[u]]\) in characteristic \(p\),
+
+\[
+ (\Theta+1)u^j=(j+1)u^j.                                 \tag{4.4}
+\]
+
+Every truncation ending before \(u^{p-1}\) is invertible, but the operator
+misses \(u^{p-1},u^{2p-1},\ldots\). Its all-depth delayed loss is infinite.
+This is a direct counterexample to promoting a low-window rank profile or an
+apparently stable defect without closing the full \(p\)-periodic recurrence.
+
+## 5. The completed-ideal bridge
+
+There are two different bridge obligations: a filtered finite-depth bridge
+for the initial residual, and a full completed-ideal bridge for the final
+solution. They should not be conflated.
+
+### Proposition 5.1 (coefficient bridge under SOURCE-30)
+
+Suppose the all-depth identity (1.7) holds and
+
+\[
+ H_a(u)=\sum_{j\ge0}h_{a,j}u^j.
+\]
+
+Then
+
+\[
+ [t^n\eta^a]\mathcal E=
+ \begin{cases}
+ h_{a,j},&0\le a\le29,\ n=s_a+6j,\ j\ge0,\\
+ 0,&\text{otherwise}.
+ \end{cases}                                             \tag{5.1}
+\]
+
+Consequently the coefficient ideal of the pristine series equation is
+exactly
+
+\[
+ I_E^+=\bigl(h_{a,j}:0\le a\le29,\ j\ge0\bigr),          \tag{5.2}
+\]
+
+and in every compatible completion
+
+\[
+ \widehat I_E^+=\overline{I_E^+}.                        \tag{5.3}
+\]
+
+At finite depth \(D\), the pristine coefficient ideal below \(D\) is exactly
+
+\[
+ I_{E,<D}^+=
+ \bigl(h_{a,j}:s_a+6j<D\bigr).                           \tag{5.4}
+\]
+
+#### Proof
+
+Substitute the \(u\)-expansions into (1.7) and use \(u=t^6\). Uniqueness of
+coefficients in \(k[[t]][\eta]\) gives (5.1), from which (5.2)--(5.4) follow.
+\(\square\)
+
+This elementary proposition is the desired raw coefficient bridge **if and
+only if SOURCE-30 is true**. Literal vanishing of all higher
+\(\eta\)-components is stronger than necessary: it is enough to prove,
+coefficient by coefficient and filtration level by filtration level, that
+every additional component belongs to the ideal (5.2), with compatible
+membership traces.
+
+### Lemma 5.2 (levelwise ideal equality gives completed equality)
+
+Let \(C\) be a separated complete linearly topologized ring with defining
+ideals \(\mathfrak a_N\), and let \(I,J\subseteq C\). If
+
+\[
+ (I+\mathfrak a_N)/\mathfrak a_N
+   =(J+\mathfrak a_N)/\mathfrak a_N\quad\text{for every }N, \tag{5.5}
+\]
+
+then
+
+\[
+                         \overline I=\overline J.         \tag{5.6}
+\]
+
+#### Proof
+
+By separated completeness,
+
+\[
+ \overline I=\bigcap_N(I+\mathfrak a_N),\qquad
+ \overline J=\bigcap_N(J+\mathfrak a_N).
+\]
+
+Equation (5.5) says \(I+\mathfrak a_N=J+\mathfrak a_N\) for each \(N\), so
+the intersections agree. \(\square\)
+
+For an emitted Schur/pivot presentation, a sufficient implementable proof of
+(5.5) is to give, at every depth, compatible matrices \(U_N,V_N\) over the
+localized chart ring such that
+
+\[
+ g_N=U_Nh_N,\qquad h_N=V_Ng_N,                            \tag{5.7}
+\]
+
+with every inverted determinant a named chart unit. Exact reconstruction and
+direct pristine-row replay is another sufficient route. Equality of radicals,
+equality of point sets, or a one-way normal-form reduction is not an ideal
+bridge.
+
+Even completed equality alone should not be used to infer the initial
+filtered residual: a presentation change can mix levels. The positive
+certificate should directly recompute (1.11), or provide a filtration-
+triangular version of (5.7). Once the Newton limit has \(H_a=0\) for all
+\(a\), Proposition 5.1 and Lemma 5.2 show that every pristine equation in the
+bridged ideal vanishes. Radical, saturation, Rabinowitsch, pivot-definition,
+and `W`-pin equations are fixed base-chart conditions, not extra tail-function
+rows; the reconstruction certificate must show that they remain satisfied.
+
+## 6. Conditional Euler/Ore lifting theorem for this campaign
+
+### Theorem 6.1 (corrected 30-stream germ certificate)
+
+Fix a coefficient field \(k\), a named radical/chart branch, `PIN42`, and all
+other held-fixed base data. Let \(s_D\) be a depth-\(D\) point. Suppose a
+certificate supplies:
+
+1. a legal full source completion \(s\) of \(s_D\);
+2. SOURCE-30 and BRIDGE-30 for the actual all-depth equation at that
+   completion;
+3. the exact residual check \(\mathcal H(s)\in F^DY^+\);
+4. the exact derivative \(L_s^+=D\mathcal H_s\), independently replayed from
+   (1.6);
+5. a causal right section
+
+   \[
+   S:F^eY^+\to X,\qquad L_s^+S=1,\qquad
+   S(F^{n+e}Y^+)\subseteq F^nX\quad(n\ge0);              \tag{6.1}
+   \]
+
+6. the integral all-depth normalization needed by Lemma 4.2; and
+7. \(D\ge2e+1\).
+
+Then there is an allowed formal tail tuple
+
+\[
+ s_\infty\in s+F^{D-e}X                               \tag{6.2}
+\]
+
+over \(k[[t]]\) satisfying the full pristine formal equation system on the
+named chart. Hence a scoped mod-\(p\) formal germ exists.
+
+#### Proof
+
+Lemma 4.2 gives (3.2)--(3.3) with \(\sigma=0\). Theorem 3.1 applied to
+\(\mathcal H\), \(L_s^+\), and (6.1) produces
+\(s_\infty=s+h_\infty\) with all 30 \(H_a\) zero and with (6.2).
+BRIDGE-30, through Proposition 5.1 and Lemma 5.2, makes every pristine formal
+equation vanish. The corrections lie only in \(X\), so all held-fixed chart
+conditions and pins remain fixed. \(\square\)
+
+The proof uses the one operator \(L_s^+\) throughout. There is no additional
+requirement that right inverses at the Newton iterates be stable.

