If a normalization audit finds a loss \(\sigma>0\), replace (7.3) by
\(\nu-2e-\sigma\ge1\), or the second inequality in (7.4) by
\(2e+\sigma+1\le D\).

## 8. What \(e^+\) should mean

The Newton theorem does not use the determinant-index diagnostic. The
mathematically sharp promoted quantity is

\[
 e^+_{\rm Newt}:=\text{a certified upper bound on the loss in (7.2)}. \tag{8.1}
\]

If the parametrix realizes the minimal loss, this is \(\ell^+\). It is safe,
and clearest, to retain two separate fields:

```text
E_LOSS_CERT     = e_Newt_plus
E_INDEX         = 6*d_plus - 12       # diagnostic only
```

The legacy conservative convention may instead set

\[
 e^+=(e^+)^{\rm idx}=6d^+-12,                            \tag{8.2}
\]

but only after an exact normal form also proves a section of loss
\(\ell^+\le e^+\). Under that convention,

\[
 0\le\ell^+\le e^+,\qquad 2e^++1\le D                  \tag{8.3}
\]

is sufficient. Merely stabilizing \(d^+\), computing (8.2), and checking a
weighted pivot sum is not sufficient. Conversely, a direct section of loss
\(e\) proves a lift using (N) even when the index is larger or unavailable.

The concrete thresholds are therefore:

| depth | sharp causal-loss condition | conservative index condition |
|---:|---:|---:|
| \(23\) | \(e^+_{\rm Newt}\le11\) | \(\ell^+\le(e^+)^{\rm idx}\le11\) |
| \(25\) | \(e^+_{\rm Newt}\le12\) | \(\ell^+\le(e^+)^{\rm idx}\le12\) |
| \(D\) | \(e^+_{\rm Newt}\le\lfloor(D-1)/2\rfloor\) | same with the certified index upper bound |

Since \((e^+)^{\rm idx}=6d^+-12\), the conservative D25 route permits only
\(d^+=2,3,4\), giving indices \(0,6,12\); D23 permits only \(d^+=2,3\).
Those integer restrictions do not replace PARAM-30.

The current finite-window lower bound \(\ell^+\ge31\) at the sampled D23
operators rules out a D23 or D25 certificate at those points. It implies only
that any threshold based on that same operator is at least \(D=63\). D63 is
sufficient only if an all-depth right section of loss at most \(31\) is also
proved; a lower bound by itself supplies no lift and does not rule out a more
singular formal germ.

At D25:

- family-wide EMPTY kills the scoped formal germ without any Newton lemma;
- NONEMPTY plus SOURCE-30, BRIDGE-30, PARAM-30 of loss at most \(12\), and the
  exact residual check proves a scoped mod-\(p\) formal germ by Theorem 6.1;
- NONEMPTY without those certificates is only finite-depth survival.

